Drop bytemuck's nightly-date-gated portable_simd impls, keeping the new-API one.

bytemuck gates its two `core::simd::Simd` impls of Pod and Zeroable on a
*nightly date* - `#[rustversion::before(2026-01-27)]` for the old API and
`#[rustversion::since(2026-01-27)]` for the new one - to track the day the
`LaneCount<N>: SupportedLaneCount` bound left core::simd. See
https://github.com/Lokathor/bytemuck/issues/343.

A date bound is the wrong question to ask. rustversion orders a stable or beta
compiler *below* every nightly date (`Stable | Beta => Ordering::Less` in
`rustversion-v1/src/bound.rs`), deliberately and upstream - the vendored crate
is pristine dtolnay, chromium does not patch it. So on a stable rustc `before`
is true and `since` is false, and the crate compiles the impl whose where-clause
names `core::simd::LaneCount<N>: core::simd::SupportedLaneCount` - neither of
which exists in rust 1.95, where `Simd<T, const N: usize>` carries no such
bound. The build then fails with "cannot find trait SupportedLaneCount in module
core::simd".

Upstream never reaches this path because chromium builds with its own
dev-channel rustc, which takes rustversion's `Dev => Ordering::Greater` arm and
therefore selects the new-API impl. OE builds with a stable rustc from
meta-lts-mixins, so it takes the other arm.

Both impls are also behind `#[cfg(feature = "nightly_portable_simd")]`, which
chromium's own `third_party/rust/bytemuck/v1/BUILD.gn` enables together with
`-Zallow-features=portable_simd`, so the gate is live and the crate really does
have to pick one.

Delete the pre-2026-01-27 impl and the `since` attribute on the other, leaving
exactly one impl - the one that matches the core::simd we build against - still
behind the crate's own feature gate. bytemuck is the only non-test vendored
crate using nightly date bounds, so nothing else changes.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [vendored crate gates on a nightly date; OE
builds with a stable rustc that already has the new core::simd API, while
upstream chromium uses a dev-channel rustc and never selects the old impl]

